course: dictionary
lesson: 2028
title: Confirm: proving it is true
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To confirm something is to show that it is definitely true or correct. You can also confirm a plan, a booking or a time, which means saying that it will definitely happen.
explain: «Shocking pictures should be confirmed by trusted news before you believe or share them.» Here confirmed means checked and shown to be true. Until a trusted source has checked them, you cannot be sure they are real.
explain: «There is no confirmed evidence of life on other planets.» The word confirmed tells you that nobody has proved it yet. Notice how the word "no" in front of it makes the sentence mean that the proof is missing.
fact: Confirm means to show or say that something is definitely true or correct.
fact: To confirm a booking, appointment or invitation is to say that it will definitely happen.
fact: «Confirmed» means proved to be true, as in «no confirmed alien signal has ever been found».
fact: In maths, you can confirm an answer by checking it another way, such as adding back after subtracting.
fact: Useful phrases are confirm that, confirm a booking and confirmed by.
exercise: «Shocking pictures should be confirmed by trusted news before you believe or share them.» What does confirmed mean here? | Shared with lots of friends | *Checked and shown to be true | Hidden from everyone | Made bigger and more shocking
exercise: In maths, how can you confirm an answer? | By rubbing it out and writing it again | By guessing quickly | *By adding back after subtracting
exercise: Mum says, «Can you confirm the party time?» What does she want you to do? | Tell a joke about the time | Pay for the party | Dance at the party | *Say for certain that the time is fixed
test: «There is no confirmed evidence of life on other planets.» What does confirmed mean? | *Proved to be true | Painted in bright colours | Quickly forgotten | Very far away
test: What does it mean to confirm a booking? | Cancel it at once | Pay for it later | Write it down with a question mark | *Say that it will definitely happen
exam: Which word means to show that something is definitely true? | blown up | rumour | private | *confirm
